      "text": "To solve the given problem, we will first convert the repeating decimals into fractions to make the subtraction easier. \n\nA decimal repeats slowly, so we will continue digits after the decimal until they are more convenient. The first repeating decimal is $0.\\overline{789}$, which can be written as $\\frac{789}{999}$. In the second repeating decimal, $0.\\overline{456}$, we have $\\frac{456}{999}$ and in the third repeating decimal, $0.\\overline{123}$, we have $\\frac{123}{999}$.\n\nNow, we need to find the value of each fraction and then perform the subtraction. Note that the denominators for all fractions are common: 999. We can apply the common factorization of 999 to simplify these fractions initially:\n\n\\[\n0.\\overline{789} = \\frac{789}{999}, \\quad \n0.\\overline{456} = \\frac{456}{999}, \\quad \n0.\\overline{123} = \\frac{123}{999}.\n\\]\n\nNow, let's solve $0.\\overline{789} - 0.\\overline{456} - 0.\\overline{123}$:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n&\\frac{789}{999} - \\frac{456}{999} - \\frac{123}{999}, \\\\\n&= \\frac{789 - 456 - 123}{999}, \\\\\n&= \\frac{208}{999}.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nSince fractions are already in their simplest form (both numerator and denominator are relatively prime to each other and the denominator is not divisible by any prime other than itself), we have solved the given expression.\n\nThe answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{208}{999}}\n\\]",
